Chapter 1. Admin Guide

1.1. Overview

   This module ships structured log entries from the Kamailio
   configuration script to a VictoriaLogs server. The entries are
   formatted as JSON lines and sent over HTTP to the VictoriaLogs

   Logging is asynchronous: the SIP worker processes only pack the values
   of the log entry and pass them to a dedicated sender process. The
   sender process serializes the entries to JSON, collects them in a queue
   and posts them in batches, so the SIP processing is never blocked by
   the HTTP transfer.

   The set of fields of a log entry is described by log groups. A log
   group is defined with the default_map and map parameters and is
   referenced by its key as the first argument of the vl_log() function.
   Fields of the default map are filled automatically (current time, route
   name, pseudo-variables, static values), fields of the map are filled
   from the arguments of vl_log().

   Several targets (VictoriaLogs instances) can be configured for high
   availability. Batches are sent to the targets in round-robin order;
   when a request fails, the batch is retried on the next target.

1.2. Dependencies

1.2.1. Kamailio Modules

   The following modules must be loaded before this module:
     * pv - needed when pseudo-variables are used in default_map or in the
       text arguments of vl_log(), which is the case in any practical
       configuration.

1.2.2. External Libraries or Applications

   The following libraries or applications must be installed before
   running Kamailio with this module loaded:
     * libcurl.

   The module is available only on Linux: the sender process is built on
   epoll and timerfd.

1.3. Parameters

1.3.1. target (str)

   URL of the VictoriaLogs jsonline ingestion endpoint, e.g.
   http://127.0.0.1:9428/insert/jsonline. The parameter can be set
   multiple times to configure several targets.

   Default value is empty (at least one target is required).

   Example 1.1. Set target parameter
...
modparam("log_victorialogs", "target", "http://127.0.0.1:9428/insert/jsonline")
modparam("log_victorialogs", "target", "http://10.0.0.2:9428/insert/jsonline")
...

1.3.2. stream_fields (str)

   Comma-separated list of field names that identify the log stream in
   VictoriaLogs. The value is passed in the VL-Stream-Fields HTTP header.
   The fields should have a small number of distinct values (host name,
   application name, log group).

   Default value is empty (all entries go to the default stream).

   Example 1.2. Set stream_fields parameter
...
modparam("log_victorialogs", "stream_fields", "host,app")
...

1.3.3. msg_field (str)

   Name of the field that VictoriaLogs should use as the log message
   (_msg). The value is passed in the VL-Msg-Field HTTP header.
   Alternatively the field can be named _msg directly in the map.

   Default value is empty (VictoriaLogs uses the _msg field).

   Example 1.3. Set msg_field parameter
...
modparam("log_victorialogs", "msg_field", "message")
...

1.3.4. time_field (str)

   Name of the field that VictoriaLogs should use as the log entry
   timestamp (_time). The value is passed in the VL-Time-Field HTTP
   header. Alternatively the field can be named _time directly in the
   default map. When no time field is present, VictoriaLogs uses the
   ingestion time.

   Default value is empty (VictoriaLogs uses the _time field).

   Example 1.4. Set time_field parameter
...
modparam("log_victorialogs", "time_field", "ts")
...

1.3.5. extra_fields (str)

   Comma-separated list of name=value pairs added by VictoriaLogs to every
   ingested entry. The value is passed in the VL-Extra-Fields HTTP header.

   Default value is empty.

   Example 1.5. Set extra_fields parameter
...
modparam("log_victorialogs", "extra_fields", "host=sip1,env=prod")
...

1.3.6. account_id (int)

   VictoriaLogs tenant account id, sent in the AccountID HTTP header
   together with project_id. The headers are only sent when at least one
   of the two is greater than zero.

   Default value is 0.

   Example 1.6. Set account_id parameter
...
modparam("log_victorialogs", "account_id", 12)
...

1.3.7. project_id (int)

   VictoriaLogs tenant project id, sent in the ProjectID HTTP header. See
   account_id.

   Default value is 0.

   Example 1.7. Set project_id parameter
...
modparam("log_victorialogs", "project_id", 34)
...

1.3.8. auth_username (str)

   User name for HTTP basic authentication on the targets. The
   authentication is enabled when both auth_username and auth_password are
   set.

   Default value is empty (no authentication).

   Example 1.8. Set auth_username parameter
...
modparam("log_victorialogs", "auth_username", "kamailio")
...

1.3.9. auth_password (str)

   Password for HTTP basic authentication on the targets. See
   auth_username.

   Default value is empty (no authentication).

   Example 1.9. Set auth_password parameter
...
modparam("log_victorialogs", "auth_password", "secret")
...

1.3.10. timeout (int)

   Maximum duration of one HTTP request in milliseconds. A request that
   takes longer is treated as failed and the batch is retried on the next
   target.

   Default value is 5000.

   Example 1.10. Set timeout parameter
...
modparam("log_victorialogs", "timeout", 2000)
...

1.3.11. batch_size (int)

   Maximum number of log entries in one HTTP request. When the number of
   queued entries exceeds this value, a request is sent immediately
   without waiting for batch_delay.

   Default value is 0 (no limit, the whole queue is sent in one request).

   Example 1.11. Set batch_size parameter
...
modparam("log_victorialogs", "batch_size", 100)
...

1.3.12. batch_delay (int)

   Interval in milliseconds between the sending of the queued entries. The
   minimum value is 100.

   Default value is 0 (100 milliseconds).

   Example 1.12. Set batch_delay parameter
...
modparam("log_victorialogs", "batch_delay", 1000)
...

1.3.13. queue_size (int)

   Maximum number of log entries kept in the queue of the sender process.
   When the limit is exceeded (e.g. all targets are down), new entries are
   dropped, an error is logged and the vl_drop_total statistic is
   incremented.

   Default value is 0 (unlimited).

   Example 1.13. Set queue_size parameter
...
modparam("log_victorialogs", "queue_size", 10000)
...

1.3.14. default_map (str)

   Fields of a log group that are filled automatically on every vl_log()
   call. The format of the value is described in the section Format of
   default_map and map parameters. The parameter can be set multiple
   times, once per log group.

   Default value is empty.

   Example 1.14. Set default_map parameter
...
modparam("log_victorialogs", "default_map", "gen(_time:timestamp, route:route_na
me, src_ip:$si, app:&kamailio)")
...

1.3.15. map (str)

   Fields of a log group that are filled from the arguments of the
   vl_log() function. The format of the value is described in the section
   Format of default_map and map parameters. The parameter can be set
   multiple times, once per log group.

   Default value is empty (at least one map is required).

   Example 1.15. Set map parameter
...
modparam("log_victorialogs", "map", "gen(_msg:text, code:num)")
...

1.4. Format of default_map and map parameters

   The value of both parameters has the format key(name:type[, name:type
   ...]).
     * key - name of the log group, used as the first argument of
       vl_log().
     * name - name of the JSON field in the log entry. The VictoriaLogs
       special fields _msg, _time and the stream fields can be used
       directly.
     * type - type of the field, see below.

   Types allowed in default_map:
     * timestamp - current time in RFC3339 format with millisecond
       precision (UTC), e.g. 2026-10-06T08:09:41.123Z.
     * route_name - name of the route block from which vl_log() is called.
     * $... - a pseudo-variable, its value is taken at the time of the
       call (e.g. $si, $ci, $var(x)). Null values result in the field
       being omitted.
     * &value - a static string value.

   Types allowed in map:
     * text - string. The argument may contain pseudo-variables, they are
       evaluated at the time of the call. Empty strings are omitted from
       the entry.
     * num - integer number.
     * float - floating point number.
     * bool - boolean, the argument must be true, false or a number
       (non-zero is true).
     * inet - IPv4 or IPv6 address, stored as a string.
     * date - date and time in local time zone in the format %Y.%m.%d
       %H:%M:%S, stored in RFC3339 format (UTC).

1.5. Functions

1.5.1.  vl_log(key[, value1, ...])

   Build a log entry of the log group key and pass it to the sender
   process. The number of values must be equal to the number of fields in
   the map of the group, and each value must match the type of the
   corresponding field. Up to 5 values can be given.

   Returns 1 on success and -1 on error (unknown group, wrong number or
   type of values, sender process not reachable).

   This function can be used from ANY_ROUTE.

   Example 1.16. vl_log usage
...
modparam("log_victorialogs", "target", "http://127.0.0.1:9428/insert/jsonline")
modparam("log_victorialogs", "stream_fields", "app,group")
modparam("log_victorialogs", "default_map", "gen(_time:timestamp, app:&kamailio,
 group:&gen, route:route_name, callid:$ci, src_ip:$si)")
modparam("log_victorialogs", "map", "gen(_msg:text, code:num)")
modparam("log_victorialogs", "default_map", "auth(_time:timestamp, app:&kamailio
, group:&auth, callid:$ci)")
modparam("log_victorialogs", "map", "auth(_msg:text, user:text, ok:bool)")
...
request_route {
    ...
    vl_log("gen", "request $rm from $si", "100");
    ...
    if (!auth_check("$fd", "subscriber", "1")) {
        vl_log("auth", "authentication failed", "$au", "false");
        auth_challenge("$fd", "1");
        exit;
    }
    vl_log("auth", "authenticated", "$au", "true");
    ...
}
...

1.6. Statistics

1.6.1. vl_queue_size

   Current number of log entries waiting in the sender queue.

1.6.2. vl_drop_total

   Total number of log entries dropped because the queue was full.

1.6.3. vl_err_total

   Total number of failed HTTP requests.

1.7. Queue algorithm

   The sender process collects the log entries in a queue. Every
   batch_delay milliseconds, or as soon as the queue grows over batch_size
   entries, the first batch_size entries of the queue (the whole queue
   when batch_size is 0) are posted to the current target in one request.
   Only one request is in flight at a time.

   On success the entries are removed from the queue and the next batch is
   sent immediately if the queue is not empty. On failure the vl_err_total
   statistic is incremented and the same batch is retried on the next
   target. When all the targets failed, the entries stay in the queue
   until the next batch_delay tick.

   When the queue holds more than queue_size entries, new entries are
   dropped and vl_drop_total is incremented.
